// Services rather than Mapping test suites because only a Service carries an explicit Runtime, and
// the chain lives in the runtime: MappingTestRunner builds its runtime purely from the test's
// store-test-data bindings, so a mapping testSuites block cannot name a ModelChainConnection.
//
// One route over the model and fixture: the relation projection, which is also the only form that
// reaches DuckDB here (RelationalConnectionFactory picks DuckDB when the query returns a Relation
// and H2 otherwise). Why the other two routes are not services is explained at the foot of this
// file. Wider coverage lives in meta::relational::tests::semistructured::modelChain, which injects
// the dialect connection directly.

###Service
// The reported shape in the relation form: project(~[...]) onto a to-many whose source array is
// only reachable after a subType cast.
//
// Firm X's second term carries no collateral key and contributes a null row. That is how
// semi-structured flattening behaves generally, not something the chain adds - the same projection
// straight against chain::mapping::Relational produces the same row.
Service chain::service::FirmHoldingsRelationService
{
  pattern: '/api/chain/firmHoldingsRelation';
  documentation: 'Collateral piece ids per firm, as a relation, reached through a subType cast in the chain.';
  autoActivateUpdates: true;
  execution: Single
  {
    query: |chain::model::Firm.all()->project(~[id: x | $x.id, name: x | $x.name, pieceId: x | $x.holdings.pieceId]);
    mapping: chain::mapping::M2M;
    runtime: chain::runtime::FirmRuntime;
  }
  testSuites:
  [
    holdingsRelationSuite:
    {
      data:
      [
        connections:
        [
          h2:
            Reference
            #{
              chain::data::FirmData
            }#
        ]
      ]
      tests:
      [
        subTypeEmbeddedArrayAsRelation:
        {
          // Without this a Service suite compares against the whole execution envelope
          // (builder, activities, result) rather than the rows.
          serializationFormat: PURE_TDSOBJECT;
          asserts:
          [
            shouldMatch:
              EqualToJson
              #{
                expected:
                  ExternalFormat
                  #{
                    contentType: 'application/json';
                    data: '[{"id":1,"name":"FIRM X","pieceId":"C1"},{"id":1,"name":"FIRM X","pieceId":"C2"},{"id":2,"name":"FIRM Y","pieceId":"C3"},{"id":3,"name":"FIRM Z","pieceId":null},{"id":1,"name":"FIRM X","pieceId":null}]';
                  }#;
              }#
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

// The TabularDataSet and graph-fetch routes over this model are deliberately not services here.
//
// A relation return type is what selects the execution target: RelationalConnectionFactory picks
// DuckDB when the query returns a Relation and H2 otherwise. relational-semistructured's mapping
// makes the same point -- the TDS form "would silently run somewhere without a SEMISTRUCTURED type
// and stop testing anything here". A TDS service over this model does reach H2 and then fails
// inside legend_h2_extension_flatten_array, which cannot read the document this model's ###Data
// block loads, while the identical query passes on both H2 and DuckDB under the PCT harness, whose
// CSV loading differs. That is an EMIT harness gap, tracked separately from this model.
//
// Graph fetch cannot read anything behind a Binding at all. Routing resolves a property's sets
// through mapping->classMappingById and a binding-mapped property registers no class mapping, so
// plan generation fails. Supporting it also needs a per-element key for array elements, which the
// relational metamodel has no notion of.
//
// Both shapes are covered, with their expected output, by
// meta::relational::tests::semistructured::modelChain: the TDS ones run on every dialect the
// parameterised suite covers, and the graph-fetch ones are parked with <<paramTest.Ignore>> as the
// acceptance criteria for implementing it.
